---
import SimulatorLayout from '../../layouts/SimulatorLayout.astro';
import BreathingSim from '../../simulators/breathing/BreathingSim.astro';
import { breathingQuiz } from '../../data/quizzes/breathing';

const toc = [
  { id: 'parts', label: 'Parts of the respiratory system' },
  { id: 'ventilation', label: 'How breathing works' },
  { id: 'exchange', label: 'Gas exchange in the alveoli' },
  { id: 'numbers', label: 'Breathing by the numbers' },
  { id: 'scenarios', label: 'Exercise, altitude and asthma' },
  { id: 'control', label: 'What controls breathing' },
  { id: 'terms', label: 'Key terms' },
  { id: 'misconceptions', label: 'Common misconceptions' },
  { id: 'takeaways', label: 'Key takeaways' },
];

const references = [
  { text: 'Betts, J. G., et al. (2022). Anatomy and Physiology 2e, Section 22.3: The Process of Breathing. OpenStax.', url: 'https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/22-3-the-process-of-breathing' },
  { text: 'Betts, J. G., et al. (2022). Anatomy and Physiology 2e, Section 22.4: Gas Exchange. OpenStax.', url: 'https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/22-4-gas-exchange' },
  { text: 'West, J. B., & Luks, A. M. (2020). West’s Respiratory Physiology: The Essentials (11th ed.). Wolters Kluwer.' },
  { text: 'Severinghaus, J. W. (1979). Simple, accurate equations for human blood O₂ dissociation computations. Journal of Applied Physiology, 46(3), 599–602.' },
];
---

<SimulatorLayout
  branch="anatomy-physiology"
  topic="respiratory-system"
  description="Interactive breathing simulator: move the diaphragm, watch the lungs expand, follow oxygen into the blood and compare rest, exercise, high altitude and an asthma attack."
  toc={toc}
  quiz={breathingQuiz}
  references={references}
>
  <Fragment slot="intro">
    <p>
      You take around 20,000 breaths a day without thinking about it. Each one is driven by muscles changing the size of
      your chest, and each one brings fresh oxygen to millions of tiny air sacs where it slips into your blood. Watch it
      happen, take control of the diaphragm, and see how exercise, altitude and asthma change things.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <BreathingSim slot="sim" />

  <Fragment slot="instructions">
    <ol>
      <li>Watch the <strong>diaphragm</strong> (red curve) move down and the <strong>ribs</strong> lift as the lungs fill, and compare with the <strong>pressure</strong> readout.</li>
      <li>Look at the <strong>alveolus</strong> on the right: oxygen (red dots) moves into the blood and carbon dioxide (grey dots) moves out.</li>
      <li>Change the <strong>Situation</strong>. Watch the <strong>blood oxygen saturation</strong>, the <strong>blood carbon dioxide</strong> and the <strong>lung volume graph</strong>.</li>
      <li>Turn on <strong>Control the diaphragm yourself</strong> and drag the round handle (or use the slider) to breathe in and out by hand.</li>
      <li><strong>Click any part</strong> of the diagram to learn what it does.</li>
    </ol>
  </Fragment>

  <h2 id="parts">Parts of the respiratory system</h2>
  <p>
    Air enters through the nose or mouth, where it is warmed, moistened and filtered. It travels down the
    <strong>trachea</strong> (windpipe), which is held open by C-shaped rings of cartilage, then into two
    <strong>bronchi</strong>, one for each lung. The bronchi branch again and again into narrower
    <strong>bronchioles</strong>, about 23 generations of branching in total, ending in clusters of
    <strong>alveoli</strong>. The lungs sit inside the rib cage, above the dome-shaped <strong>diaphragm</strong>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ventilation">How breathing works</h2>
  <p>
    The lungs have no muscles of their own. Breathing in (<strong>inspiration</strong>) happens when the
    <strong>diaphragm contracts</strong> and flattens, and the <strong>external intercostal muscles</strong> pull the ribs
    up and out. The chest gets bigger, the lungs expand with it, and the pressure inside them falls slightly below the air
    pressure outside. Air flows in until the pressures are equal.
  </p>
  <p>
    Quiet breathing out (<strong>expiration</strong>) is mostly passive: the diaphragm relaxes and domes upwards, the
    stretchy lungs recoil, the pressure inside rises slightly above atmospheric, and air flows out. During exercise,
    internal intercostal and abdominal muscles help push air out faster. The simulator shows the pressure inside the lungs:
    just one or two centimetres of water below or above atmospheric is enough to move half a litre of air.
  </p>

  <h2 id="exchange">Gas exchange in the alveoli</h2>
  <p>
    The two lungs contain roughly 480 million alveoli with a total surface area of about 70 m², the size of a small
    apartment. Each alveolus is wrapped in capillaries, and the barrier between air and blood is less than a thousandth of
    a millimetre thick. Gases move by <strong>diffusion</strong>, from high to low concentration (more precisely, partial
    pressure). Oxygen diffuses from the alveolar air into the blood and binds to <strong>haemoglobin</strong> in red blood
    cells; carbon dioxide diffuses from the blood into the alveoli to be breathed out.
  </p>

  <h2 id="numbers">Breathing by the numbers</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Tidal volume:</strong> air moved in a normal breath, about 500 mL at rest.</li>
    <li><strong>Breathing rate:</strong> about 12–20 breaths per minute in a resting adult.</li>
    <li><strong>Minute ventilation:</strong> rate × tidal volume, about 6–8 L per minute at rest and over 100 L per minute in hard exercise.</li>
    <li><strong>Dead space:</strong> about 150 mL of each breath stays in the airways and never reaches the alveoli.</li>
    <li><strong>Oxygen saturation:</strong> usually 95–100 % in healthy people at sea level.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="scenarios">Exercise, altitude and asthma</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Exercise:</strong> working muscles produce much more carbon dioxide, so breathing becomes faster and deeper to
    remove it and to supply extra oxygen. <strong>High altitude:</strong> air is still 21 % oxygen, but the air pressure is
    lower, so there is less oxygen in each breath; saturation falls even though breathing speeds up, and over weeks the
    body adapts by making more red blood cells. <strong>Asthma:</strong> the airways become inflamed and the muscles around
    them tighten, raising resistance to airflow. Breathing out is hardest, so air becomes trapped and the lungs stay
    over-inflated.
  </p>

  <h2 id="control">What controls breathing</h2>
  <p>
    Breathing is controlled automatically by the <strong>medulla</strong> in the brainstem. Chemoreceptors constantly
    monitor carbon dioxide (and the acidity it causes) in the blood. A rise in carbon dioxide is the main stimulus to
    breathe harder, which is why you cannot hold your breath for very long.
  </p>

  <h2 id="terms">Key terms</h2>
  <dl>
    <dt>Ventilation</dt>
    <dd>Moving air in and out of the lungs.</dd>
    <dt>Alveoli</dt>
    <dd>Microscopic air sacs where gas exchange happens.</dd>
    <dt>Partial pressure</dt>
    <dd>The share of the total gas pressure due to one gas, such as oxygen.</dd>
    <dt>Haemoglobin</dt>
    <dd>The iron-containing protein in red blood cells that carries oxygen.</dd>
  </dl>

  <h2 id="misconceptions">Common misconceptions</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>“The lungs pull air in.”</strong> The diaphragm and rib muscles enlarge the chest; the lungs follow.</li>
    <li><strong>“We breathe out only carbon dioxide.”</strong> Exhaled air is still about 16 % oxygen and mostly nitrogen.</li>
    <li><strong>“Breathing and respiration are the same thing.”</strong> Breathing is ventilation; respiration is the chemical process in cells that releases energy.</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="callout callout-warn">
    <p>
      <strong>Educational model only.</strong> The values in this simulator are simplified textbook estimates. It cannot
      assess breathing problems, asthma or oxygen levels in a real person and is not medical advice. If you or someone else
      has difficulty breathing, seek medical help immediately. See our <a href="/disclaimer/">educational and medical
      disclaimer</a>.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="takeaways">Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Breathing in: diaphragm contracts and ribs lift, the chest gets bigger, pressure falls, air flows in.</li>
    <li>Gas exchange happens by diffusion across the thin walls of the alveoli.</li>
    <li>Ventilation = breathing rate × tidal volume.</li>
    <li>Lower air pressure (altitude) or narrowed airways (asthma) reduce how well oxygen reaches the blood.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="muted">Model note: lung mechanics use a single resistance and compliance; gas values use the alveolar gas equation and a standard oxygen–haemoglobin curve. Real lungs vary from region to region.</p>
</SimulatorLayout>
